Definition 2.10 (Underlying homotopy type and core) Let \(\mathcal S=\mathbf{Types}\) and let \[
i\colon\mathcal S\hookrightarrow\mathbf{Cat}_{\infty,\infty}
\] be the full inclusion of groupoidal higher categories. Assume the adjunctions \[
\Pi_\infty\dashv i\dashv(-)^\simeq.
\] The left adjoint \[
\Pi_\infty\colon\mathbf{Cat}_{\infty,\infty}\longrightarrow\mathcal S
\] is the underlying homotopy type obtained by inverting every morphism. The right adjoint \(C\mapsto C^\simeq\) is the core obtained by retaining every object and only the equivalences (Lurie 2018, Tags 01DQ and 02F5).


Definition 2.11 (Limits, loops, and suspensions) The higher category \(\mathbf{Cat}_{\infty,\infty}\) has an initial object \(\varnothing\) and a terminal object \(*\), and it is bicomplete. For a pointed higher category \((X,x)\), where \(x\colon *	o X\), define \[
\Omega_xX:=*	imes_X*.
\] For a higher category \(A\), define its suspension by the pushout
















































The two coprojections \(*	o\Sigma A\) make \(\Sigma A\) bipointed.


Definition 2.12 (Spheres) Define \[
S^{-1}:=\varnothing,
\qquad
S^{n}:=\Sigma S^{n-1}\quad(n\geq0).
\] Since the pushout of \(*\leftarrow\varnothing	o*\) is the coproduct, \(S^{0}=*\amalg*\), the bipointed object indexing the comma category of Definition 2.14.


Example 2.13 (The initial and terminal higher categories) Closedness makes \(-	imes C\) a left adjoint (Definition 2.5), so it preserves colimits and in particular the initial object: \[
\varnothing	imes C\simeq\varnothing,
\qquad
*	imes C\simeq C,
\qquad
\varnothing\amalg C\simeq C.
\] For hom-objects, \([\varnothing,C]\simeq*\) for every \(C\), since \(\varnothing\) is initial; \([C,\varnothing]\simeq\varnothing\) whenever \(C\) has a point, since evaluating a point of \([C,\varnothing]\) at a point of \(C\) would give a point of \(\varnothing\), and a higher category with no points is \(\varnothing\); and \([\varnothing,\varnothing]\simeq*\). The coproduct \(*\amalg*\) is neither initial nor terminal, so \(\mathbf{Cat}_{\infty,\infty}\) has no zero object.


Definition 2.14 (The $B_{01}$ construction) The higher category of bipointed higher categories is the comma category \[
((*\amalg *)\downarrow\operatorname{id}_{\mathbf{Cat}_{\infty,\infty}}).
\] Its objects are morphisms \[
x_0\amalg x_1\colon *\amalg *\longrightarrow X.
\] The endpoint hom functor is \[
\Omega_{01}\colon
((*\amalg *)\downarrow\operatorname{id}_{\mathbf{Cat}_{\infty,\infty}})
\longrightarrow\mathbf{Cat}_{\infty,\infty},
\qquad
(X;x_0,x_1)\longmapsto[x_0,x_1]_X.
\] Assume that \(\Omega_{01}\) has a left adjoint \[
B_{01}\dashv\Omega_{01}.
\] For a higher category \(A=\int_*h_A\), the bipointed higher category \(B_{01}A\) has distinguished objects \(0,1\) and hom-objects displayed by





















































Composition is given by the identity actions on \(A\) and the unique morphisms from \(\varnothing\). The suspension \(\Sigma A\) is the pushout in Definition 2.11. The object \(B_{01}A\) is characterized by the adjunction \(B_{01}\dashv\Omega_{01}\).


Definition 2.15 (Walking arrows and ordinal categories) Define \[
[0]:=*,
\qquad
[1]:=B_{01}*.
\] Write \(s,t\colon[0]	o[1]\) for the two distinguished objects. For each integer \(n\geq2\), define the ordinal category \([n]\), the walking chain of \(n\) composable arrows, by \[
[n]:=
\underbrace{[1]\amalg_{[0]}[1]\amalg_{[0]}\cdots\amalg_{[0]}[1]}_{n	ext{ copies}},
\] where each pushout identifies the target of one copy with the source of the next. For a higher category \(C\), the hom-object \[
[[n],C]_{\mathbf{Cat}_{\infty,\infty}}
\] is the higher category of functors \([n]	o C\) and their higher cells.


Definition 2.16 (Walking-arrow presentation and mapping types) Precomposition with \(s\) and \(t\) gives \[
(s^*,t^*)\colon
[[1],C]_{\mathbf{Cat}_{\infty,\infty}}
\longrightarrow
[[0],C]_{\mathbf{Cat}_{\infty,\infty}}
