Remark 40.1 We record the lattice-theoretic constructions attached to a group of isometries of a lattice, and specialize them to the case of an involution. This apparatus underpins the lattice theory of the K3-cover / Enriques-cover picture used throughout: the \(2\)-elementary lattices \(S_\Co\) and \(T_\Co\) arise as the invariant and coinvariant lattices of the nonsymplectic involution on the K3 cover of a Coble surface, and it is these lattices that enter the chain of primitive embeddings of the embedding-sequence lemma (Lemma 48.2).


Definition 40.2 Let \(G\injects \Orth(L)\) be a faithful representation realizing a group \(G\) acting on a lattice \(L\) by isometries. The invariant sublattice and the coinvariant sublattice of the action are defined as \[
L^G \definedas 	heset{v\in L \mid g.v = v 	ext{ for all } g\in G},
\qquad
L_G \definedas (L^G)^{\perp L}
.
\] Thus \(L^G\) is the sublattice of vectors fixed by every element of \(G\), and \(L_G\) is its orthogonal complement in \(L\).


Proposition 40.3 Suppose \(G = \generators{I}\) is generated by an involution \(I\in\Orth(L)\), so that \(I^2 = \id\). Then, for the induced action of \(I\) on \(L_\QQ\), the rational spans \((L^G)_\QQ\) and \((L_G)_\QQ\) are precisely the eigenspaces of \(I\) for the eigenvalues \(+1\) and \(-1\) respectively, and \[
L_\QQ = (L^G)_\QQ \operatorname{\perp} (L_G)_\QQ
.
\]


Proof. Since \(I^2 = \id\), the induced isometry of \(L_\QQ\) is diagonalizable with all eigenvalues in \(	heset{+1, -1}\), so \(L_\QQ = V_+ \oplus V_-\) where \(V_\pm\) denotes the \(\pm 1\) eigenspace. The fixed space \(V_+\) is by definition \((L^G)_\QQ\). For \(v\in V_+\) and \(w\in V_-\), using that \(I\) is an isometry, \[
 eta_{L_\QQ}(v, w) =  eta_{L_\QQ}(I v, I w) =  eta_{L_\QQ}(v, -w) = - eta_{L_\QQ}(v, w)
,
\] so \( eta_{L_\QQ}(v, w) = 0\). Hence \(V_-\iscontainedin V_+^{\perp}\), and comparing dimensions gives \(V_- = V_+^{\perp} = (L_G)_\QQ\).


Proposition 40.4 Let \(G\injects\Orth(L)\) be a faithful representation of a finite cyclic group \(G\). Then \(L^G\) and \(L_G\) are primitive sublattices of \(L\), and the orthogonal direct sum \(L^G \oplus L_G\) is a finite-index sublattice of \(L\) (Nikulin 1980).


Proposition 40.5 Let \(L\) be an even nondegenerate lattice and let \(G = \generators{I}\cong \ZZ/2\ZZ\) be generated by an involution \(I\in\Orth(L)\). The invariant and coinvariant sublattices \(L^G\) and \(L_G\) are primitive and mutually orthogonal. Moreover, each \(v\in L\) decomposes orthogonally, up to a factor of \(2\), as \[
2\cdot v = v_+ + v_-,
\qquad
v_+ \definedas v + I(v)\in L^G,
\qquad
v_- \definedas v - I(v)\in L_G
.
\]


Proof. Orthogonality and primitivity are the case \(G = \generators{I}\) of the primitivity proposition (Proposition 40.4), and \(L_G = (L^G)^{\perp L}\) is orthogonal to \(L^G\) by definition. For the decomposition, fix \(v\in L\) and set \(v_+ \definedas v + I(v)\) and \(v_- \definedas v - I(v)\), both of which lie in \(L\). Applying \(I\) and using \(I^2 = \id\), \[
I(v_+) = I(v) + I^2(v) = I(v) + v = v_+,
\qquad
I(v_-) = I(v) - v = -v_-
,
\] so \(v_+\) is fixed by \(I\) and hence \(v_+\in L^G\), while \(v_-\) lies in the \(-1\) eigenspace of \(I\) and hence, by the eigenspace proposition (Proposition 40.3), in \((L_G)_\QQ\intersect L = L_G\). Adding the two expressions gives \(v_+ + v_- = 2v\), and the summands are orthogonal since \(v_+\in L^G\) and \(v_-\in L_G = (L^G)^{\perp L}\).


Proposition 40.6 Let \(L\) be a \(2\)-elementary lattice and let \(I\in\OStab(L)\) be an involution lying in the stable orthogonal group \(\OStab(L) = 	ilde\Orth(L)\). Then both the invariant lattice \(L^I\) and the coinvariant lattice \(L_I\) are again \(2\)-elementary.


Proof. Write \(\pi_\pm\colon L	ensor_\ZZ\QQ 	o L^{\pm}	ensor_\ZZ\QQ\) for the projections \(\pi_\pm(v) = 	frac12(v \pm I v)\), where \(L^{+} = L^I\) and \(L^{-} = L_I\).
Both \(L^{\pm}\) are primitive in \(L\): if \(nv \in L^I\) for some \(v \in L\) and \(n 
eq 0\) then \(nIv = nv\), so \(Iv = v\); and \(L_I = (L^I)^{\perp}\) is primitive because an orthogonal complement always is. A primitive sublattice is a direct summand as a \(\ZZ\)-module, so restriction \(\dualof{L} 	o \dualof{(L^{\pm})}\) is surjective, and under the identifications made by the form it is \(\pi_{\pm}\). Hence every \(x \in \dualof{(L^{\pm})}\) is \(x = \pi_{\pm}(w)\) for some \(w \in \dualof{L}\), and \[
2x = w \pm Iw .
\]
Now use the two hypotheses. Since \(I\) lies in the stable orthogonal group it acts trivially on \(A_L = \dualof{L}/L\), so \(Iw - w \in L\); and since \(L\) is \(2\)-elementary, \(2\dualof{L} \iscontainedin L\), so \(2w \in L\). Therefore \[
2x = w - Iw = -(Iw - w) \in L
\qquad	ext{and}\qquad
2x = w + Iw = 2w + (Iw - w) \in L
\] in the two cases respectively. In each case \(2x\) lies in \(L^{\pm}	ensor_\ZZ\QQ\) as well as in \(L\), and \(L^{\pm}\) is primitive, so \(2x \in L^{\pm}\).
Thus \(2\dualof{(L^{\pm})} \iscontainedin L^{\pm}\), which is \(2\)-elementarity.





