Lemma 48.1 Writing
\[
 egin{aligned}
T_\Co = \generators{2} \oplus E_{10}(2) &= \generators{h, e', f',\alpha_1,\cdots, \alpha_8} \
T_\En = U \oplus E_{10}(2) &= \generators{	ilde e,	ilde f,	ilde e',	ilde f',	ilde \alpha_1,\cdots,	ilde \alpha_8},
\end{aligned}
\]
there is an embedding of lattices \(T_\Co \injects T_\En\):
\[
 egin{aligned}
\eta: \generators{2} \oplus E_{10}(2) &	o U \oplus E_{10}(2) \
(h, x) &\mapsto (	ilde e + 	ilde f, x)
\end{aligned}
\]
which sends the generator \(h\) of \(\generators{2}\) to \(	ilde e+	ilde f\in U\) and is the identity on the \(E_{10}(2)\) summand. Since \(\coker \eta\) is torsionfree, \(\eta\) is a primitive embedding.


Lemma 48.2 There is a sequence of primitive embeddings
\[
T_{\Co} \injects 	en \injects 	dp \injects \lkt
\]
which is unique up to \(\Orth(\lkt)\). In particular, this yields an embedding
\[
 egin{aligned}
\generators{2} \oplus U(2) \oplus E_8(2) &\injects U \oplus U(2) \oplus E_8^2 \
(h,x,y) & \mapsto (	ilde e + 	ilde f, x, y, y)
\end{aligned}
\]
and thus an embedding \( co \injects F_{(2,2,0)}\).


Proof. By (Alexeev et al. 2025 Lem. 2.4), it suffices to show uniqueness of \(S_{\En} \injects S_{\Co}\), i.e.
\[
E_{10}(2) \injects \generators{-2}\oplus E_{10}(2)
,
\]
or equivalently by untwisting,
\[
E_{10} \injects \generators{-1}\oplus E_{10}
.
\]
This embedding is unique because \(E_{10} = U \oplus E_8\) is unimodular. A primitively embedded unimodular sublattice splits its ambient lattice (the unimodular-splitting proposition (Proposition 38.12)), so the codomain of any primitive embedding of \(E_{10}\) is \(E_{10}\oplus E_{10}^{\perp}\). The gluing datum of such an embedding is the graph of an isometry between a subgroup of \(A_{E_{10}}\) and a subgroup of \(A_{E_{10}^\perp}\) (the embedding-gluing description (Remark 38.10)), and \(A_{E_{10}} = 0\), so that datum is trivial and the embedding is determined by the isometry class of the complement. Similarly, by (Nikulin 1980 Cor. 1.5.2, Thm. 3.6.3), the homomorphism \(\Orth(\lkt)	o \Orth(T_\Co)\) is surjective.


Lemma 48.3 The embeddings of lattices \(\eta: T_\Co\injects T_\En\) (resp. \(T_{\Co} \injects 	dp\)) induce locally closed embeddings \( co \injects  en\) (resp. \( co \injects F_{(2,2,0)}\)) which extend to morphisms on the Baily–Borel compactifications.


Proof. This follows from (Kiernan and Kobayashi 1972, secs. 5, Thm.2).


Theorem 48.4 \( co\) is the normalization of a closed subvariety of \( en\).


Proof. Write \(\Gamma_\Co\definedas\Orth^+(T_\Co)^*\) and \(\Gamma_\En\definedas\Orth^+(T_\En)^*\), and let \[
f\colon  co =  D(T_\Co)/\Gamma_\Co 	oo  D(	en)/\Gamma_\En =  en
\] be the map induced by the embedding \(\eta\) of the primitive-embedding lemma (Lemma 48.1). The argument has four steps: a gluing computation that identifies the stabilizer of \(T_\Co\), finiteness of the fibres, properness, and generic injectivity.
The gluing datum. Put \(K\definedas \eta(T_\Co)^{\perp T_\En}\). Since \(\eta(h) = 	ilde e+	ilde f\) and \(\eta\) is the identity on the \(E_{10}(2)\) summand, \(K\) is the orthogonal complement of \(	ilde e+	ilde f\) in \(U = \generators{	ilde e,	ilde f}\), so \(K = \ZZ k\) with \(k\definedas	ilde e-	ilde f\) and \(k^2 = -2\). Thus \(K\cong\generators{-2}\), and \(A_K\) is generated by \(k/2\) with \(q_K(k/2) = -1/2\). Because \(	ilde e =  rac12 igl(\eta(h)+k igr)\), the overlattice \(T_\En\) of \(T_\Co\oplus K\) has gluing group \[
H = \generators{ igl(h/2,\; k/2 igr)}\ \leq\ A_{T_\Co}\oplus A_K
\] in the sense of the gluing-datum definition (Definition 43.4). It has order \(2\) and is isotropic, since \(q_{T_\Co}(h/2)+q_K(k/2) =  rac12- rac12 = 0\).
Two features of \(A_{T_\Co} = \generators{h/2}\oplus A_{E_{10}(2)}\) are used below. First, \(A_{E_{10}(2)}\) is orthogonal to \(h/2\). Second, \(q_{T_\Co}(h/2) =  rac12\), whereas \(q_{T_\Co}\) takes values in \(\ZZ/2\ZZ\) on \(A_{E_{10}(2)}\) because \(E_{10}\) is even and unimodular.
The stabilizer. We claim that restriction to \(T_\Co\) carries \(\Stab_{\Gamma_\En}(T_\Co)\) onto \(\Gamma_\Co\).
Let \(g\in\Gamma_\En\) satisfy \(g(T_\Co) = T_\Co\). Then \(g\) preserves \(K = T_\Co^{\perp}\) as well, so \(g = \varphi\oplus\varepsilon\) with \(\varphi\definedasestrictionof{g}{T_\Co}\in\Orth(T_\Co)\) and \(\varepsilon = \pm 1\) on \(K\). By the automorphism-lifting criterion (Theorem 43.5), \(g\) preserves \(T_\En\) only if \(( ar\varphi\oplus ar\varepsilon)(H) = H\). The group \(A_K\) has order \(2\), so \( ar\varepsilon = \id\), and the condition on the generator of \(H\) reads \( igl( ar\varphi(h/2),\,k/2 igr)\in H\). The only element of \(H\) with second coordinate \(k/2\) is the generator, so \[
 ar\varphi(h/2) = h/2
.
\] Next let \(x\in A_{E_{10}(2)}\). Then \((x,0)\in H^{\perp}\), because \(x\) is orthogonal to \(h/2\). By Nikulin’s gluing theorem (Theorem 38.9) the discriminant group of \(T_\En\) is \(H^{\perp}/H\), and \(g\) lies in the stable orthogonal group, so \(g\) fixes that quotient pointwise; hence \( igl( ar\varphi(x)-x,\,0 igr)\in H\). Every nonzero element of \(H\) has second coordinate \(k/2
eq 0\), so \( ar\varphi(x) = x\). The two displays give \( ar\varphi = \id\) on all of \(A_{T_\Co}\), that is \(\varphi\in	ilde\Orth(T_\Co)\). Finally \(g\) preserves the component \( D(T_\En)\), and \( D(T_\Co) =  D(T_\En)\intersect\PP(T_\Co	ensor_\ZZ\CC)\), so \(\varphi\) preserves \( D(T_\Co)\) and lies in \(\Orth^+(T_\Co)\). Therefore \(\varphi\in\Gamma_\Co\).
Conversely let \(\varphi\in\Gamma_\Co\) and put \(g\definedas\varphi\oplus\id_K\). Both \( ar\varphi\) and \( ar{\id}_K\) are the identity, so \(g\) preserves \(H\) and extends to \(T_\En\) by the automorphism-lifting criterion (Theorem 43.5). The extension acts trivially on \(H^{\perp}/H = A_{T_\En}\) and preserves \( D(T_\En)\), so it lies in \(\Gamma_\En\); it stabilizes \(T_\Co\) and restricts to \(\varphi\) there.
In particular the containment required by the descent criterion (Remark 48.6) holds, with equality, so \(f\) is defined.
Fibres. For \(x = [\omega]\in D(T_\En)\) let \(T(x)\) be the smallest primitive sublattice of \(T_\En\) whose complexification contains \(\omega\), namely \(T(x)\definedas igl(T_\En\intersect\omega^{\perp} igr)^{\perp}\). For a primitive sublattice \(M\leq T_\En\) of signature \((2,\ast)\) one has \(x\in D(M)\) if and only if \(T(x)\iscontainedin M\).
Fix \(y\in F_\En\) and a lift \(	ilde y\in D(T_\En)\). A point of \(\inverseof{f}(y)\) is a \(\Gamma_\Co\)-orbit of a point \(x = g	ilde y\) with \(g\in\Gamma_\En\) and \(x\in D(T_\Co)\). Writing \(M'\definedas \inverseof{g}(T_\Co)\), that last condition says \(T(	ilde y)\iscontainedin M'\). Now \(M'^{\perp} = \inverseof{g}(K) = \ZZ v\) with \(v^2 = -2\), and \(v\perp T(	ilde y)\). The space \(T(	ilde y)	ensor_\ZZ\RR\) contains a positive definite plane and \(T_\En\) has signature \((2,10)\), so \(T_\En\intersect T(	ilde y)^{\perp}\) is negative definite and contains only finitely many vectors of square \(-2\). Each such \(v\) determines \(M' = v^{\perp}\), so only finitely many \(M'\) occur. For a fixed \(M'\) the elements \(g\) with \(\inverseof{g}(T_\Co) = M'\) form one coset of \(\Stab_{\Gamma_\En}(T_\Co)\), whose restrictions to \(T_\Co\) all lie in \(\Gamma_\Co\) by the previous step; the corresponding points \(x\) therefore form a single \(\Gamma_\Co\)-orbit. Hence \(\inverseof{f}(y)\) is finite, of cardinality at most half the number of \((-2)\)-vectors of \(T_\En\intersect T(	ilde y)^{\perp}\).
Properness and finiteness. By the Baily–Borel extension lemma (Lemma 48.3) the map \(f\) extends to a morphism \( ar f\colon\overline{F_\Co}^{\operatorname{BB}}	o \overline{F_\En}^{\operatorname{BB}}\) of projective varieties, which is therefore proper. A boundary component of \(\overline{F_\Co}^{\operatorname{BB}}\) is indexed by a primitive isotropic sublattice \(I\leq T_\Co\) of rank \(1\) or \(2\), and the saturation of \(I\) in \(T_\En\) is again primitive isotropic of the same rank. So \( ar f\) carries boundary to boundary, giving \(\inverseof{ ar f}( en) =  co\), and \(f\) is proper because properness is stable under base change. A proper morphism with finite fibres is finite (The Stacks project authors 2025) [Tag 02LS], so \(f\) is finite. Let \(Z\definedas f( co)\), which is closed in \( en\) because a finite morphism is closed.
Generic injectivity. A proper primitive sublattice \(S\subsetneq T_\Co\) of signature \((2,\ast)\) cuts out a proper closed analytic subset \( D(S)\subsetneq D(T_\Co)\), and there are countably many such \(S\). So the set of \(x\in D(T_\Co)\) with \(T(x) = T_\Co\) is the complement of countably many proper closed analytic subsets, hence dense. For such an \(x\) the fibre computation allows only \(M' = T_\Co\): one has \(T_\Co = T(x)\iscontainedin M'\) with both primitive of rank \(11\). The fibre through \(x\) is therefore a single point. The locus where a finite morphism of irreducible varieties has more than its generic number of preimages is a proper closed subset, and a dense set is not contained in one, so \(f\colon F_\Co	o Z\) has degree \(1\) and is birational.
Finally \(F_\Co\) is normal, being the quotient of a bounded symmetric domain by a properly discontinuous group of automorphisms. A finite birational morphism from a normal variety onto a reduced irreducible variety is its normalization, so \(F_\Co\) is the normalization of \(Z\).


Remark 48.5 (The same statement one level up) The normalization theorem (Theorem 48.4) is the Coble analogue of (Alexeev et al. 2025 Lem. 2.8), which asserts that \( entwo\) is the normalization of a closed subvariety of \(F_{(2,2,0)}\). There the stabilizer step is run through \(T_\dP\): the isometry group \(\Orth(T_\dP)\) is the image of \(	heset{g\in\Orth(\lkt)\mid g(T_\dP) = T_\dP,\; g(h) = h}\), because the order-two group \(\Orth(q_{S_\dP})\) preserves the class of \(	frac12 h\) in \(A_{S_\dP}\cong A_{U(2)}\cong(\ZZ/2)^2\), and the stabilizer of \(T_\En\) in \(\Gamma_\dP\) is then \(\Gamma_{\En,2}\). The proof above replaces that detour by the direct gluing computation, which is available here because \(T_\Co^{\perp T_\En}\cong\generators{-2}\) is of rank one.


Remark 48.6 (What the stabilizer statement has to supply) The stabilizer step of the normalization theorem (Theorem 48.4) is one instance of a general criterion for descending a map to a pair of quotients. Let \(f\colon A\injects B\) be an inclusion of sets, and let \(G_A\) and \(G_B\) be groups acting on \(A\) and on \(B\). Then \(f\) descends to a map of orbit spaces \[
 ar f\colon G_A ackslash A 	o G_B ackslash B
\] precisely when every element of \(G_A\) acts on \(A\) as the restriction of some element of \(G_B\) preserving \(f(A)\), that is when \[
\im igl(G_A	o\Aut(A) igr)
\;\iscontainedin\;
\im igl(\Stab_{G_B}(f(A))	o\Aut(f(A)) igr)
.
\] For the case at hand, \(A =  D(T_\Co)\) with \(G_A = \Orth^+(T_\Co)^*\) and \(B =  D(T_\En)\) with \(G_B = \Orth^+(T_\En)^*\), so what is needed is that every isometry of \(T_\Co\) in \(G_A\) extends to an isometry of \(T_\En\) preserving \(T_\Co\). The criterion is a containment, not an equality: for a general pair of groups the stabilizer of \(f(A)\) in \(G_B\) may restrict to a group strictly larger than the image of \(G_A\) without obstructing the descent. What a strictly larger restriction costs is injectivity, since two \(G_A\)-orbits may then be identified in \(G_B ackslash B\). The gluing computation in the proof above rules that out here: the restriction of \(\Stab_{G_B}(T_\Co)\) to \(T_\Co\) is exactly \(\Orth^+(T_\Co)^*\), so the containment is an equality and the only source of non-injectivity left is a point of \( D(T_\Co)\) lying on a second translate \(\inverseof{g}(T_\Co)\). That is what the fibre count controls.





