Remark 60.1 (On Morrison's degenerations) As explained in (Morrison 1981) and the appendix of (Shah 1981), a flower is a pair \((\PP^2, C)\) where \(C^2 = 4\) is a conic, while a pot is a pair \((V, D)\) where \(V\) is a rational surface and \(D = \Sum C_i\) is a sum of rational curves satisfying \(C_i\cdot C_j = -4\delta_{ij}\). A stalk is a pair \((\Sigma_4, s_0 + s_\infty)\) where \(\Sigma_4\) is a rational ruled surface with sections \(s_0, s_\infty\) satisfying \(s_0^2 = 4\) and \(s_\infty^2 = -4\). A stalk assembly is a flower glued to some number of stalks along boundary double curves, and a flower pot is a pot glued to stalk assemblies along double curves.

Question 60.1.1 Check that this is the Hirzebruch surface \(F_4\) with only part of its toric boundary.

By (Morrison 1981 Lem. 7.1, 7.2), some degenerations of Coble surfaces correspond to flower pot degenerations \(\mathcal{X}\), since the period of central fiber \(\mathcal{X}_0\) of such a degeneration is contained in the Coble divisor \(\mathcal{H}_{-2} \iscontainedin  en\) and \(2K_{\mathcal{X}}
eq 0\). By (Alexeev et al. 2025), flowers are the semistable resolutions of the \(S_2\)-quotients of the ADE singularities in the total space of the smoothing. The pots are components of the dlt models of (Alexeev et al. 2025) along which flowers and stalks are attached. Flower pots correspond to type \(\mathrm{(i.b)}\) in the classification (Morrison 1981 Cor. 6.2), which by (Alexeev et al. 2025 Rmk. 4.12) are Type \(\latI\) semistable degenerations of Enriques surfaces with a klt singularity.





Alexeev, Valery, Philip Engel, D. Zack Garza, and Luca Schaffler. 2025. “Compact Moduli of Enriques Surfaces with a Numerical Polarization of Degree 2.” Nagoya Mathematical Journal.


Morrison, David R. 1981. “Semistable Degenerations of Enriques’ and Hyperelliptic Surfaces.” Duke Mathematical Journal 48 (1): 197–249. https://doi.org/10.1215/S0012-7094-81-04813-4.


Shah, Jayant. 1981. “Projective Degenerations of Enriques’ Surfaces.” Mathematische Annalen 256 (4): 475–95. https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01450543.

