Remark 61.1 
We recall the mirror move algorithm from (Alexeev and Engel 2022). We have Nikulin’s 2-elementary diagram:














































































































































































































































































Figure 61.1: White nodes are \(\delta=0\), black are \(\delta=1\), double circled are \(\delta = 0,1\).





61.0.1 Mirror moves
Having identified the 2-elementary lattice \(S_\Co = (11, 11, 1)_1\), one can apply the mirror move algorithm of (Alexeev and Engel 2022 Thm. 5.10) to determine the 0-cusps and 1-cusps of \(F_\Co\). The outcome of the algorithm is summarized by the following tree:




















































































































































































































































































































Figure 61.2: Blue (resp. red) indicate lattices which are valid (resp. invalid) targets of mirror moves.



Thus \(F_\Co\) has one 0-cusp corresponding to an isotropic vector \(v_0\) with
\[
v_0^{\perp T_{\Co}}/\generators{v_0} \cong (9,9,1)_1 \cong \generators{2} \oplus E_{8}(2)
\]
Moreover, this 0-cusp \(v_0\) is incident to one 1-cusp \(C_0\) corresponding to an isotropic plane \(J = \generators{v_0, v_1}\) with
\[
J^{\perp T_{\Co}}/\generators{J} \cong (7,7,1)_0 \cong A_1^{\oplus 7} ,
\]
where \(v_1 \in v_0^{\perp T_{\Co}}/\generators{v_0}\). In the diagrammatic language of (Alexeev and Engel 2022, fig. 1, Thm. 5.10), this corresponds to a \(U^2\) move and can be summarized in the following mirror move diagram as a composition of two even ordinary \(U(2)\)-type moves:

















































































































































































Figure 61.3: The mirror moves for \(S_{\Co} = (11,11,1)_1\).



Note that \(v_0\) corresponds to a Type \(m{III}\) boundary, while \(C_0\) corresponds to a type \(m{II}\) boundary. It is easily verified that the Coxeter diagram \(G_{(9,9,1)_1}\) at \(v_0\) has precisely one maximal parabolic subdiagram, corresponding to a finite-index root lattice of type \(B_7\). We note that by (Alexeev and Engel 2022, sec. 5), such isotropic vectors are unique up to \(\Orth(T_\Co)\), and so we can choose representatives:

\(v_0 = e'\),
\(v_1 = 2h + \alpha_1 + \alpha_2\).

Calculations verify that \(v_0^2 = v_1^2 = 0\), that \(v_1 \in v_0^{\perp T_{\Co}}/\generators{v_0}\), and that \(v_0v_1 = 0\), and thus \(J \definedas \generators{v_0, v_1}\) is an admissible choice of an isotropic plane. We further note that \(\div_{T_{\Co}}(v_0) = \div_{T_{\Co}}(v_1) = 2\), which will be an important invariant for establishing a correspondence to cusps of other moduli spaces. For an isotropic plane \(J\), we denote the divisibilities of the constituent generating vectors as a tuple \((d_1, d_2)\), and in this convention we have \(\div_{T_{\Co}}(v_0, v_1) = (2, 2)\).
By (Cossec et al. 2025 Prop. 5.4.6), there is an open embedding \(F_{(11, 11, 1)} \injects F_{(10,10,0)}\), i.e. \( co \injects  en\), realizing \( co\) as the coarse space of marked Coble surfaces with \(n=1\), where \(n\) is the number of boundary components in \(C = C_1 + \cdots + C_n\). The image is an open subset of a closed irreducible subset of \(\mch_{-2}/\Gamma_\En\). By (Cossec et al. 2025 Thm. 5.8.2), the coarse space of \( co\) is a rational variety, and since \( en\) is quasiprojective, so too is \( co\). Moreover, \(\partial \overline{ en}^{\mathrm{BB}}\) consists of \( co\) and two modular curves \(X\) and \(X_0(2)\) by (Cossec et al. 2025 Thm. 5.9.8), and the closure of \(\mch_{-2}\) contains the modular curve \(X\).

