Theorem 63.1 The embedding \(\eta: F_\Co	o F_\En\) induces the correspondence on boundary cusps of the Baily-Borel compactifications shown in Figure 63.1.























































































































































































































































































































































































































































































Figure 63.1: Cusp correspondence \(F_\Co 	o F_\En\).



We prove the cusp correspondence by comparing divisibilities of isotropic vectors at the corresponding 0-cusps and 1-cusps in both moduli spaces.

Lemma 63.2 Any \(v\in T_\Co\) satisfies \(\div_{T_\Co}(v) = 2\) if there exists any \(v'\in T_\Co\) for which \(v\cdot v' 
eq 0\).


Proof. Let \(M_{T_\Co}\) be the Gram matrix for \(T_\Co\), and note that since
\[
T_\Co = \generators{2} \oplus E_{10}(2) = \qty{\generators{1} \oplus E_{10}}(2)
,
\]
every entry in \(M_{T_\Co}\) is 2-divisible. Thus one can write \(M_{T_\Co} = 2A\) for some integral matrix \(A\). Letting \(v' \in T_\Co\) be any other vector not orthogonal to \(v\), we have
\[
v\cdot v' = \inner{v}{M_{T_\Co}v'} = \inner{v}{2 Av'}
= 2\inner{v}{Av'} 
eq 0
,
\]
where \(\inner{\cdot}{\cdot}\) is the standard Euclidean inner product. Thus 2 divides \(v\cdot v'\), and since \(\div_{T_\Co}(v) \in 	heset{1, 2}\) for any \(v \in T_\Co\), the result follows.


Lemma 63.3 Fixing notation,
\[
 egin{aligned}
v_1 &\definedas e' & w_1 &\definedas \eta(v_1) = 	ilde e' \
v_2 &\definedas 2h + \alpha_1 + \alpha_2 & w_2 &\definedas \eta(v_2) = 2	ilde e + 2	ilde f + 	ilde\alpha_1 + 	ilde\alpha_2 \
J &\definedas \generators{v_1, v_2} & 	ilde J &\definedas \generators{w_1, w_2}
\end{aligned}
\]
We then have \(\div_{T_\En}(w_1) = 2\).


Proof. By the divisibility lemma (Lemma 63.2), we have in particular that \(v_1\) has divisibility 2 in \(T_\Co\); moreover it is isotropic. The group \(\Orth^+(T_\Co)\) is transitive on primitive isotropic vectors, with quotient \(\latI_{1,8}(2)\) at any of them (the unpolarized-cusp theorem (Theorem 85.27), the split maximal hypothesis being the split-maximal proposition (Proposition 41.4)), so \(v_1\) represents the unique full-group \(0\)-cusp of \(F_\Co\) and \[(v_1)^{\perp T_\Co}/v_1 \cong \latI_{1,8}(2) = (9,9,1) \cong \generators{2} \oplus E_8(2).\] Whether the \(\Gamma_\Co\)-orbits coincide with the \(\Orth(T_\Co)\)-orbits is the residue recorded among the open problems; the argument below uses only the full-group statement.
Since \(	ilde e' 	ilde f' = 2\), and \(	ilde e'\) is orthogonal to the remaining generators of \(T_\En\), we have
\[
\div_{	en}(w_1) \definedas \div_{	en}(\eta(e'))
= \div_{T_\En}(	ilde e') = 2.
\]
Explicitly, writing \(	en = U \oplus U(2) \oplus E_8(2)\), one can check the pairing of \(	ilde e'\) against an arbitrary vector. Write \(x+y+z\in U \oplus U(2)\oplus E_8(2)\); then
\[
	ilde e'\cdot(x+y+z) = 	ilde e'\cdot y\in 	heset{0, 2}
\]
since \(y\in U(2)\) and \(	ilde e'\in (U\oplus E_8(2))^{\perp 	en}\). Thus \( eta_{	en}(	ilde e', 	en) =  eta_{	en}(	ilde e', U(2)) = 2 Z\) since \(	ilde e'\cdot	ilde f' = 2\).


Lemma 63.4 The 0-cusp \((9,9,1)_1\) in \( co\) maps to the 0-cusp \((10, 8, 0)_1\) in \( en\).


Proof. The cusp correspondence follows from computing the lattice \(w_1^{\perp 	en}/w_1\) under the primitive embedding \(\eta\), since \(\eta(v_1) = w_1\) and \(v_1\) is the isotropic vector corresponding to \((9,9,1)_1\) in \( co\):
\[
{(	ilde e')^{\perp 	en} \over \generators{	ilde e'}} =
{
\generators{	ilde{e}, 	ilde{f}}
\oplus
\generators{	ilde{e}'}
\oplus
\generators{	ilde{\alpha}_1, \dots, 	ilde{\alpha}_8}
\over
\generators{ 	ilde{e}' }}\cong\generators{	ilde{e}, 	ilde{f}} \oplus\generators{
	ilde{\alpha}_{1}, \dots, 	ilde{\alpha}_{8}
} \cong U \oplus E_{8}(2) \cong (10,8,0)_1.
\]
Alternatively, by (Alexeev and Engel 2022 Prop. 5.5), the isomorphism type of \(w_1^{\perp 	en}/w_1\) is determined by \(\mathrm{div}_{	en}(w_1)\); by the divisibility calculation (Lemma 63.3) \(\mathrm{div}_{	en}(w_1) = 2\). Since the divisibility of the isotropic vector at the Enriques 0-cusp \((10, 8, 0)_1\) is also 2 and the two Enriques 0-cusps are distinguished by divisibility, the correspondence follows.


Lemma 63.5 The 1-cusp \((7,7,1)_0\) in \(F_\Co\) maps to the 1-cusp \((8, 6, 0)_0\) in \(F_\En\).


Proof. The group \(\Orth^+(T_\Co)\) is transitive on primitive isotropic planes, and for any such plane \(J^{\perp T_\Co}/J\cong\latI_{0,7}(2)\cong A_1^{\oplus 7} = (7,7,1)\) (the unpolarized-cusp theorem (Theorem 85.27) and the split-maximal proposition (Proposition 41.4)). It remains to identify the isomorphism type of the image \(	ilde J^{\perp T_\En}/	ilde J\) in \(T_\En\). One checks that both \(v_2\) and \(w_2\) are isotropic, and \(v_2 \in v_1^{\perp T_\Co}/v_1\), and so \(J\) and \(	ilde J\) define isotropic planes in \(T_\Co\) and \(T_\En\) respectively. By (Alexeev and Engel 2022 Prop. 5.5, Lem. 5.9), it suffices to show
\[
\div_{w_1^{\perp T_\En} /w_1}(w_2) \definedas \div_{U \oplus E_8(2)}(2	ilde e + 2	ilde f + 	ilde \alpha_1 + 	ilde \alpha_2)= 2
,
\]
since the isomorphism type of \(	ilde J^\perp/	ilde J\) is uniquely determined by the isomorphism type of \(w_2^{\perp T_\En}/w_2\) in \(w_1^{\perp T_\En}/w_1\), which is in turn uniquely determined by the characterization of \(w_2\) as odd, even ordinary, or even characteristic in \(w_1^{\perp T_\En}/w_1\), which by the \(w_1^\perp\) calculation (Lemma 63.4) is isomorphic to \(U \oplus E_8(2)\). One checks directly in coordinates: let \(x+y\in U \oplus E_8(2)\) and consider its pairing with \(w_2\):
\[
(x+y)\cdot w_2
= (x+y)\cdot (2	ilde e + 2	ilde f + 	ilde \alpha_1 + 	ilde \alpha_2 )
= 2x\cdot (	ilde e + 	ilde f) + y\cdot (	ilde \alpha_1 + 	ilde \alpha_2)
,
\]
where we’ve used orthogonality relations. We note that the first term is evidently even, while the second term is even because all pairings are either zero or divisible by two in the \(E_8(2)\) summand of \(U \oplus E_8(2)\).


Remark 63.6 This can also be proved by considering the divisorial presentation of \( co\) as \(\mch_{-2}/\Orth^+(	en)^*\). If \(J'\) is an isotropic plane in \(	en\) generated by \(x_1, x_2\) where both \(x_i\) are orthogonal to a \((-2)\)-vector in \(	en\), then the 1-cusp corresponding to \(J'\) will be contained in the closure of \(\mch_{-2}\). One checks that cusp \((8, 6, 0)_0\) is associated to the isotropic plane
\[
J' = \generators{e', 2e + 2f + 2 ar\alpha_1} \subset 	en
\]
where both \(e'\) and \(2e + 2f + 2 ar\alpha_1\) are orthogonal to the \((-2)\)-vector \(e-f\in 	en\). On the other hand, cusp \((8,8,0)\) does not satisfy this property – the vector \(e\in 	en\) is not orthogonal to any \((-2)\)-vectors in \(	en\), and the isotropic plane at this cusp must include \(e\) as a generator.


Remark 63.7 The two 1-cusps \((8,8,0)\) and \((8,6,0)\) in \( en\) are isomorphic to the modular curves \(X_0(2)\) and \(X \definedas \overline{ H / \SL_2( Z)}\) respectively, and by (Cossec et al. 2025 Cor. 5.9.10) the 1-cusp \((7,7,1)\) in \( co\) is isomorphic to \(X\). This can additionally be verified by (Alexeev and Engel 2022 Prop. 5.13): the 1-cusp \((7,7,1)\) in \(T_\Co\) is incident to exactly one 0-cusp, as is the 1-cusp \((8,6,0)\) in \(	en\), and thus the corresponding modular curves are both isomorphic to \(X\). We conjecture that general correspondences on 1-cusps must preserve the isomorphism types of the corresponding modular curves, yielding an alternative proof of the one-cusp correspondence lemma (Lemma 63.5).


Lemma 63.8 Let \(	ilde w_i\) be the images of \(v_i\) in \(	dp\) under the embedding described in the sequence-of-embeddings lemma (Lemma 48.2). Then
\[
 egin{aligned}
	ilde w_1^{\perp 	dp}/	ilde w_1 &\cong (18, 0, 0)_1 \cong U \oplus E_8^2 \
	ilde w_2^{\perp 	dp}/	ilde w_2 &\cong (16, 0, 0)_0
\end{aligned}
\]
The first identification is forced: \((18,0,0)_1\) is the unique even unimodular lattice of its (indefinite) signature. The second lattice is even, unimodular, and negative-definite of rank \(16\), and is thus determined only up to the two such isometry classes.
Thus the embedding \( co \injects F_{(2,2,0)}\) induces the following maps on cusps:
\[
 egin{aligned}
    (9, 9, 1)_1 &\mapsto (18, 0, 0)_1 \
    (7, 7, 1)_0 &\mapsto (16, 0, 0)_0
\end{aligned}
\]


Proof. The map on 0-cusps follows from identifying \(	ilde w_1 = e' \in 	dp = \generators{e,f} \oplus \generators{e', f'}\oplus \generators{\alpha_1,\cdots,\alpha_8}\oplus \generators{	ilde \alpha_1,\cdots, 	ilde \alpha_8}\) and noting
\[
 egin{aligned}
{ (e')^{\perp 	dp} \over e'} &\cong {\generators{e,f}
\oplus \generators{e'}\oplus \generators{\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_8}\oplus \generators{	ilde
\alpha_1,\dots, 	ilde \alpha_8} \over e'} \ &\cong \generators{e,f} \oplus
\generators{\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_8}\oplus \generators{	ilde \alpha_1,\dots, 	ilde
\alpha_8} \ &\cong U \oplus E_8^2.
\end{aligned}
\]
To determine the map on 1-cusps, it suffices to determine \[\div_{U\oplus E_8^2}(	ilde w_2), \qquad 	ilde w_2 = 2e + 2f + \alpha_1 + \alpha_2 + 	ilde \alpha_1 + 	ilde \alpha_2.\]
However, observing that \(	ilde w_2\cdot \alpha_3 = 1\), we immediately obtain that the divisibility is one. Thus \(	ilde w_2\) is odd, and we apply case (a) of (Alexeev and Engel 2022 Thm. 5.10).


Proposition 63.9 The negative-definite lattice \(	ilde w_2^{\perp T_\dP}/	ilde w_2 \cong (16,0,0)_0\) of the del Pezzo cusp-map lemma (Lemma 63.8) is isometric to \(D_{16}^+\), and not to the other even negative-definite unimodular lattice of rank \(16\), namely \(E_8^{\oplus 2}\).


Proof. The invariants \((r,a,\delta) = (16,0,0)\) do not by themselves determine the isometry class, since the even negative-definite unimodular lattices of rank \(16\) are exactly \(E_8^{\oplus 2}\) and \(D_{16}^+\). The root system of \(E_8^{\oplus 2}\) has two orthogonal components, each of rank \(8\); the root system of \(D_{16}^+\) is the irreducible system \(D_{16}\). So it suffices to exhibit a chain of roots of \(	ilde J^\perp/	ilde J\), where \(	ilde J = \generators{	ilde w_1, 	ilde w_2}\), in which consecutive roots are not orthogonal and which spans a sublattice of rank greater than \(8\).
Label the simple roots of each \(E_8\) summand as in Bourbaki, so that \(\alpha_1 \perp \alpha_2\), as \(	ilde w_2^2 = 0\) requires, and \(\alpha_3\) meets \(\alpha_1\), as in the proof above. Put \(s = \alpha_3 + \alpha_4\) and \(	ilde s = 	ilde\alpha_3 + 	ilde\alpha_4\). The twelve vectors
\[
	ilde\alpha_8,\ 	ilde\alpha_7,\ 	ilde\alpha_6,\ 	ilde\alpha_5,\ 	ilde s - f,\ 	ilde\alpha_1 + f,\ 	ilde\alpha_1 + e,\ s - f,\ \alpha_5,\ \alpha_6,\ \alpha_7,\ \alpha_8
\]
have square \(-2\) and are orthogonal to \(	ilde w_1 = e'\) and to \(	ilde w_2 = 2e + 2f + \alpha_1 + \alpha_2 + 	ilde\alpha_1 + 	ilde\alpha_2\). Consecutive vectors in the list pair to \(\pm 1\). Since \(	ilde J\) is isotropic, it lies in the radical of the form on \(	ilde J^\perp\), so the images in \(	ilde J^\perp/	ilde J\) are roots with the same pairings. The twelve vectors together with \(	ilde J\) span a sublattice of rank \(14\), so the images span a sublattice of rank \(12\). The images therefore lie in one irreducible component of rank at least \(12\), and \(	ilde J^\perp/	ilde J 
ot\cong E_8^{\oplus 2}\).
An enumeration in Sage agrees: \(	ilde J^\perp/	ilde J\) has \(480\) roots, forming one irreducible system.


Remark 63.10 By the cusp correspondence in (Alexeev et al. 2025), this matches cusp \((9,9,1)_1\) in \(T_\Co\) with Sterk cusp 2 as the folding of \((18,0,0)_1\) by the horizontal symmetry of its Coxeter diagram. This means that the corresponding IAS and Kulikov models will be disc type, and correspond to the folding involution of Sterk 2.





